WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.451
API serving introduces concurrency.

2
00:00:02.451 --> 00:00:11.872
Instead of one user waiting on one response, the system may receive many requests with different prompt lengths, generation limits, and urgency.

3
00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:17.004
Batch tuning becomes a scheduling problem as much as a raw performance problem.

4
00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:24.586
The service has to decide how many requests to accept, how much work to group together, and when to apply backpressure.

5
00:00:24.586 --> 00:00:29.029
Continuous batching and dynamic batching are common serving ideas.

6
00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:38.986
At a high level, they let the runtime group active requests in ways that improve hardware utilization while requests arrive and finish at different times.

7
00:00:38.986 --> 00:00:43.582
This can improve throughput, but it does not remove latency tradeoffs.

8
00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:48.254
Users still care about queue delay, first-token latency, and timeouts.

9
00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:57.598
Concurrency limits, maximum prompt size, and maximum generated tokens are part of batch tuning because they shape the work entering the system.

10
00:00:57.598 --> 00:01:03.036
Without limits, a few very large prompts can consume memory and delay everyone else.

11
00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:04.415
Fairness matters.

12
00:01:04.415 --> 00:01:12.457
A service should avoid letting one request pattern dominate the queue or make normal interactive requests feel unreliable.

13
00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:15.368
Operational controls complete the picture.

14
00:01:15.368 --> 00:01:18.968
Rate limiting protects the service from demand spikes.

15
00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:24.789
Backpressure tells clients to slow down instead of silently building an unhealthy queue.

16
00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:30.916
Monitoring should show queue depth, active sequences, token rates, errors, and resource use.

17
00:01:30.916 --> 00:01:38.116
The best throughput configuration is not just fast; it is understandable and recoverable when demand changes.

18
00:01:38.116 --> 00:01:42.099
For API platforms, define the service promise before tuning.

19
00:01:42.099 --> 00:01:50.907
Decide whether the priority is low latency for interactive users, high throughput for batch jobs, or a controlled balance between both.

20
00:01:50.907 --> 00:01:56.269
Those goals lead to different queue limits, timeout values, and batching choices.

21
00:01:56.269 --> 00:02:01.707
Without that service promise, a faster benchmark can still produce a worse product.
